ded — my model is indel-free), mismatches ≤3.\n     Hmm wait — what if the read has 1–2 indels? Skip; the simulation probably has no indels (read 1 was substitution-only).\n  4. Split candidate: top 2 diagonals (d1 with votes ≥2, d2 with votes ≥2, d1≠d2): build split matrix over a∈[16,91], mismatches ≤2; pick the best (a,d1,d2).\n     Actually, to be safe, also try pairs where one diagonal has votes ≥3 and the other ≥1? A junction read with a left anchor of 20bp: left seeds: offset 0 only (since 15 > a-16=4? wait, offset 15 seed covers read[15:31]; if a=20, that seed crosses the junction → doesn't hit) → 1 vote on the left diagonal. Right diagonal gets 4–5 votes. So the pair (≥1, ≥3) matters! Generalize: for split search, take top diagonal dA (votes ≥2) and any other diagonal dB (votes ≥1), try both orders (d1,d2)=(dA,dB) and (dB,dA)? The order matters: the left diagonal should have smaller... hmm, actually in a forward-strand alignment, d1 (left) < d2 (right) (intron is positive). Try both orderings anyway, but require intron length >0 and < 100kb.\n     \n     Cost: top diagonals per read ≤ ~4; pairs ≤ 12; matrix construction per pair (76×107) is trivial. No problem.\n\n  5. Ambiguity: if a read passes both contiguous and split (unlikely), prefer the one with fewer mismatches.\n  6. Reads passing neither → unmapped (probably a background false positive).\n\nRecord contig: (idx, strand, pos, mm). Record junction: intron 1-based (s,e) = (d1+a+1, d2+a), strand, count, split position, mm.\n\nStrand note: strand=+1 means read aligns to the forward strand as-is; strand=-1 means the RC aligns to the forward strand (the original read came from the minus strand).\n\nOK, let's write probe9. For debugging, also record per-junction split position distribution and read IDs. Save results to pickle.\n"}}
