{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"reasoning","text":"Hmm, something is off. Results:\n- contig: only 1147 (the earlier fast path found 5504!)\n- Junctions: 125, with top junctions at 19.3M and 133.3M regions\n- Unresolved: 14603\n\nWait, why did the contig count drop from 5504 to 1147? Because probe9 only processed the \"keep\" set (16,088 reads) — reads that failed the fast path. The fast-path reads (5504) weren't included in keep… no wait: keep = reads where h1 or h2's mx>=3. Fast-path reads should also have mx≥2 on h1/h2… hmm, h1/h2 count hits only for seeds with ≤20 copies. Fast-path reads have unique seeds so they should be counted. So why are only 1147 contig in keep?\n\nOh wait — I see a bug in probe9's resolve(): the contig check requires diagonal vote v>=3 (`if v<3: break`). A contig read with 2 mismatches, one in each of 2 seeds, will have 5 votes — no problem. But the bigger issue is: `resolve` is only called if `h1[idx].max()>=3 or (h1[idx]>=1).sum()>=3`. h1 counts hits per seed. For a contig read with unique seeds and 0 mismatches: all 7 seeds hit → max=7, sum=7. Should pass.\n\nHmm wait, but actually — why are 14,608 unresolved? And why is contig only 1147 out of ~16k? Let's think: keep contains reads where mx>=3 in either direction — including background reads with spurious hits on repetitive kmers (e.g., a read with 3 seeds hitting a repetitive AT-rich kmer on 3 different diagonals — each diagonal gets 1 vote). Those won't resolve → unresolved. That explains most of the unresolved: keep has ~16k, real chr9 reads maybe ~7-8k… hmm.\n\nBut the contig count: 1147 from the keep set + 5504 from the fast path ≈ 6651 total contig. But wait — the fast path required both end seeds to be unique and hit with an exact offset. Reads with one end-seed error fell into keep and were resolved here. 1147 seems low but plausible.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle issue: probe9's resolve() contig branch requires top diagonal v≥3. Junction reads