{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"reasoning","text":"Currently, 8519 contiguous reads + 850 junction keys. Top junctions cluster in the following regions:\n- 87525377-87525379 — 2bp intron?! Intron length = 87525379-87525377 = 2bp. This is suspicious — likely a microhomology artifact (a read with a 2bp \"intron\" is probably a misaligned read with a 2bp deletion or tandem duplication). Not a real splice junction (in real splicing, introns are typically ≥30bp, and the minimum known is around 30-50; U12-type can be shorter but still ≥~40). Exclude introns shorter than 20bp.\n- 19.2-19.38 Mb region: junctions (19376388,19376492), (19376649,19378364), (19378514,19378706), (19378918,19379485), plus a large one (19200908,19379485) [178kb intron?!], (19200580,19378364). Both strands.\n- 133.349-133.351 Mb: multiple junctions, both strands.\n- 111.66-111.67 Mb: junctions (111661713,111664533), (111664588,111666812), (111666947,111669268) — chain of junctions, both strands.\n- 127.448-127.451 Mb: chain of junctions, both strands.\n- 124.858-124.861 Mb, 125.239-125.240, 83.97, etc.\n\nThe presence of both strands at the same coordinates strongly suggests mapping to repetitive elements: e.g., a read originating from a consensus Alu sequence could map equally well to both strands at loci where palindromic Alu pairs exist... Actually, more likely is this: these junction clusters are artifacts where a read originating from a repeat consensus aligns to many loci; my code counts all of them (up to 20 copies per seed) and votes diagonally. Hmm, but the diagonal consistency requirement is strict... For repeat-derived reads, all copies share the same sequence, so a read would align to every copy with the same \"intron\" pattern only if the genomic structure (exon-intron) is identical across copies — which doesn't apply to Alu (they're inserted at different locations). But wait — what if the read originates from a duplicated gene region?\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe these are real paralogous/segmental-duplication regions on chr9. But both strands at exactly the same intron coordinates?? For the same intron (s,e), reads aligning on the + strand and reads aligning on the - strand: the + read's sequence = ref[s-a..] + ref[..e+...] spliced together; the - read's sequence = RC of another spliced product. For both to exist with ≤3 mismatches, the locus would need to produce sense and antisense spliced transcripts sharing exact breakpoints — or it's an artifact.\n\nActually wait, there's a simpler explanation: if the intron is short and the flanks are repetitive, the same read sequence could align to both strands at the same location — e.g., a SINE dimer with inverted copies: the spliced pattern of the exonized Alu on one strand could look like an inverted-copy spliced pattern on the opposite strand. Hmm.\n\nLet me check which genes overlap these regions and look at the strand distribution of the contig reads around these loci. The real target: a highly expressed cryptic exon with two novel junctions. The true gene is probably the one with abundant contiguous coverage across the whole gene body + junctions consistent with one strand.\n\nApproach: for each region, examine contiguous read coverage (from 8519 contigs) along the genome — build a depth track and visualize the regions. Also examine strand consistency: for each junction, compare the strand of its supporting reads against the local contig-read strand.\n\nAlso the 2bp intron (87525377-87525379) is clearly a misalignment (probably a read with a 2bp deletion or tandem duplication; or alignment ambiguity at a homopolymer). Apply a minimum intron length filter (≥30? let's check the distribution of intron lengths in the annotation: Ensembl introns are typically ≥~50bp... actually the shortest human introns are around 30-50bp; U12-type AT-AC can be shorter). For novelty assessment, the exact tuple comparison handles it; for the cryptic exon search, require both novel junctions to have reasonable intron lengths (≥20bp) and canonical GT-AG.\n\nAlright, let me build a depth track and inspect candidate regions. For each of the 16 clusters, also determine the gene from the annotation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more: maybe the dual-strand pattern is real: the simulation may include antisense transcripts or readthrough. Hmm. But more likely: these regions are segmental duplications on chr9 (chr9 is known to have large segmental duplications, especially around 9q and the pericentromeric region). E.g., 111.6 Mb... hmm, is chr9:111.6 Mb near the q31-q32 region? And 124.8, 127.4, 133.3 are in the q34 region — chr9q34 has segmental duplications. 19.2-19.4 Mb is in the p21-p13 region... chr9p has a large segmental duplication block (9p12, ~40-45 Mb?). Hmm.\n\nKey insight: reads originating from a segmentally-duplicated exon would map equally well to all copies → my code would assign them to multiple loci (up to 20 per seed), producing parallel junction calls with similar counts at all copies, on both strands if the copies are in inverted orientation. This perfectly explains the pattern! E.g., a junction with ~14-26 reads appearing at multiple loci in both orientations.\n\nSo many of these junction clusters are multi-mapping artifacts from segmental duplications. A true cryptic exon junction should map uniquely (a cryptic exon and its exact junction sequence exist at only one locus).\n\nHow to handle: mark reads as ambiguous if the seeds hit many positions (currently cap 20). Better: for each resolved junction/contig alignment, count the number of equally-good candidate placements. If the read's seeds supported multiple consistent diagonals at different loci, it's multi-mapping → exclude from counting (or count with a fractional weight).\n\nLet me quantify: in resolve(), I take the top diagonal for contigs; if multiple diagonals pass verification with the same mm → ambiguous. For split: count the number of (d1,d2,a) solutions with mm≤3 → ambiguous if >1... but note: multiple split positions a for the same junction (same s,e) is fine (different breakpoints but the same intron — actually the same junction); multiple distinct (s,e) solutions = ambiguous.\n\nHmm wait, but actually there's a cleaner signal: for a segmentally-duplicated region, the contig reads would also multi-map. My contig resolver takes the best diagonal among the top 4 votes — for a duplicated exon, votes would be split across copies, each around 1-2 votes... they wouldn't pass v≥3. Unless the copies are nearly identical and seeds hit ≤20 positions each, then the votes would sum across copies? No — votes are per diagonal (per position). 2 copies → 2 diagonals, each with v≈3-4 (7 seeds split into 2 groups... no wait — each seed hits both copies → each diagonal gets ~7 votes!). Because all seeds of the read hit both copies. So both diagonals get v=7, and the contig check passes on both; my code takes the best (first) and records one alignment. Hmm, so duplicated contig reads get randomly assigned to one of the copies — inflating coverage equally across copies.\n\nAnd for junction reads within the duplication: each seed hits both copies → diagonals d1a,d1b (left segment copies) and d2a,d2b (right segment copies) → pairs (d1a,d2a) and (d1b,d2b) both verify with the same mm → my code takes the first best → again randomly assigned. Plus cross-pairs (d1a,d2b) would produce weird chimeric introns (huge or negative) — filtered by intron length constraints unless the copies are nearby.\n\nSo the observed pattern — similar junction counts on both strands at multiple loci — fits a segmental duplication with inverted copies. To resolve properly, I should count the number of valid placements per read and exclude multi-mappers.\n\nLet me redo the quantification with ambiguity tracking:\n- Contigs: count the number of diagonals with mm≤3 (within the top candidates, but I should scan all diagonals with v≥2). Ambiguous if ≥2 loci pass.\n- Junct