{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I've got 8,396 contiguous reads + 1,467 junction reads spanning 720 clusters. The same junctions keep showing up on both strands — a clear sign of segmental duplication with copies in inverted orientation. Examples:\n- 133349042-133349549: -30 / +9 (with +1 shift: 133349043-133349550 +9)\n- 19376388-19376492: +27 / -9\n- 111666947-111669268: +20 / -16\n- etc.\n\nHmm, wait. But there's something interesting here: the canonical selection picked different shifts for different strands. For example, for 133349042-133349549 the minus strand is canonical (CT-AC on the forward strand), while the plus-strand cluster picked 133349043-133349550 (which should be GT-AG on the forward strand). Both are \"canonical\" on their respective strands — this is exactly the hallmark of an inverted duplication: a spliced exon junction within a duplicated segment where one copy is forward and one is inverted. Reads from both copies map to the same coordinate region and report the junction on their own strand.\n\nSo these top junctions are almost certainly all segmental-duplication artifacts — reads from a multi-copy gene family that can't be uniquely placed. My cluster logic only marks a read as ambiguous when multiple clusters coexist within that read's placement; but reads from copy A map only to copy-A-like diagonals… no wait. Hmm, let me think again.\n\nIf a read comes from copy A of a duplicated exon-exon junction, and copies A and B are 99% identical: the read's seeds hit both copies (each seed has 2 positions, ≤20 OK). Diagonals: d1A, d1B (left segment), d2A, d2B (right segment). Valid pairs: (d1A,d2A) mm=0, (d1B,d2B) mm=1 or so (copy B differs slightly), and maybe chimeric pairs too. If mm is equal → two clusters → ambiguous → discarded. If one copy's pair is mm=0 and the other mm≥1 → the best-mm filter keeps only the true copy → uniquely assigned. So the reads being counted here are ones where one copy happens to match better.\n\nThat means the counts for these duplication junctions are roughly proportional to the expression of the underlying gene family, split between copies. These are \"real\" expressed genes, just not uniquely mappable.\n\nNow — the task: find a highly expressed cryptic exon with two novel junctions. Among all these junctions, which ones are within a protein-coding gene and which are novel relative to the annotation? The top clusters look like constitutive exon-exon junctions of highly expressed genes (annotated → not novel). The cryptic-exon junctions are novel ones with moderate support, paired in the same region.\n\nStrategy going forward:\n1. Load the annotation → intron set + gene models (chr9).\n2. Classify each junction cluster: annotated (present in the intron set) or novel.\n3. Look for pairs of novel junctions in proximity, with the interval between them covered by contiguous reads → cryptic exon.\n\nAlso, I should check for novel junctions in the long tail too. Let me annotate all 720 clusters. Furthermore, I need to build a coverage track from the 8,396 unique contiguous reads for expression evidence.\n\nBut wait — I'm worried about reads that map uniquely but whose placement was determined by a slight mm difference in a duplicated region — those could still be misassigned between copies (e.g., a read from copy B assigned to copy A because of a sequencing error that happened to match A). For cryptic exon detection, the cryptic exon sequence + its junctions are unique (the cryptic exon is at a single locus), so the reads supporting them should map uniquely with mm=0-1.\n\nLet me look for novel junctions now. First, load the annotation and build:\n- intron_set: (chrom, start, end) 1-based inclusive across all transcripts (all chromosomes).\n- chr9 gene models: gene symbol, strand, exons, introns, span.\n\nThen for each junction cluster (all on chr9): check whether (s,e) is in the intron set → annotated vs. novel. List the novel ones sorted by support. Also check the dinucleotides (the canonical selection already guarantees GT-AG/CT-AC at the chosen coordinates, but double-check the non-canonical fallbacks)."}}
