 between CE and exonB). CE = left_intron_end+1 .. right_intron_start-1.\n\nIn Region B: if CE is 19376493..19376648 (156bp), then J_left=(19376388,19376492) [27 reads], J_right=(19376649,19378364) [4 reads]. Hmm, J_right has only 4 reads versus 27 for J_left — asymmetric. Alternative: CE = 19378365..19378513 (149bp): J_left=(19376649,19378364) [4 reads], J_right=(19378514,19378706) [14 reads]. Also asymmetric.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider: this looks like a whole chain of novel exons — like a transcript from a gene model that's not in Ensembl at all?? Or... the entire region might be a simulated \"gene\" with an annotated structure, and my annotation comparison is failing due to chromosome naming (the annotation's '9' vs 'chr9' — I used ('9',s,e) — the annotation's chrom column is '9' so that's correct).\n\nHmm, wait a sec. Let me look at what genes are at these positions on chr9. 19.37-19.38 Mb: this is... let me check gene_info around there. And 133.35 Mb. Let me also check the annotation exons/introns around those regions. Maybe the simulated transcript uses exon boundaries that are slightly different (shifted by ±1-2bp) from Ensembl — e.g., if the simulation used a different annotation version (e.g., GENCODE or RefSeq), then even the \"constitutive\" junctions would be novel relative to Ensembl 112! That would explain why the top novel junctions with 20-30 reads form chains: they're just regular exons of the simulated gene annotated with slightly different coordinates.\n\nHmm, that complicates the \"novelty\" assessment, but the task says novelty is evaluated against the provided annotation version. OK.\n\nBut the cryptic exon itself: the \"highly expressed cryptic exon supported by two novel junctions.\" Among these regions, which interval is the cryptic exon? Criteria:\n1. The two novel junctions flank it.\n2. The exon body is highly expressed: contiguous reads covering the interval.\n3. The interval is inside a protein-coding gene.\n\nSo let me check the coverage track for these regions. Build depth from 8,396 unique contiguous reads, and output the depth profile around each candidate region. Also check which genes overlap.\n\nAlso, for each region, check the annotation structure: what Ensembl transcripts/exons are there.\n\nLet me also reconsider the strand ambiguity within regions: Region B has J3 on both strands (+14, -12), J4 also on both strands. Region A (133.35 Mb) is minus-dominant. If a region corresponds to a gene in Ensembl, then junctions on the gene's strand are more trustworthy.\n\nWait, actually — I should compare the junction coordinates to the introns of the genes in these regions: the \"top annotated junctions\" list includes e.g. (133350053,133350239,+1, 18 reads) — annotated on the + strand; but the Region A junctions 133349042-133349549 are novel on the - strand. So there's an annotated gene on the + strand around 133.35 Mb, plus a novel minus-strand chain. Let me look up genes at 133.34-133.36 Mb and 19.37-19.38 Mb.\n\nNext probe: for each candidate region, output:\n- Overlapping Ensembl genes (chr9), strand, exon coordinates\n- Depth profile from contiguous reads (in bins)\n- Junction chain with strand\n\nLet me write it."}}
