{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I can see the big picture! Let me analyze each region:\n\n**Region 19.37-19.38 Mb: Genes DENND4C (strand +, 19230435-19374281) and RPS6 (strand -, 19375715-19380236).**\nInteresting point: RPS6 is on the minus strand, spanning 19375715-19380236. The junction chain 19376388→19379485 is inside RPS6! Depth: 19376500-19376999: 3918; 19378000-19378499: 3074; 19378500-19378999: 4095. Very high depth! RPS6 is ribosomal protein S6 — extremely highly expressed.\n\nWait, but the junctions show up on both strands... RPS6 is on the minus strand. Minus-strand junctions: (19376388,19376492,-1,9 reads), (19378514,19378706,-1,12), (19378919,19379486,-1,14), (19376649,19378364,-1,2). Plus-strand junctions: (19376388,19376492,+1,27), (19378514,19378706,+1,14), (19378918,19379485,+1,14), (19376649,19378364,+1,4). Hmm — both strands have substantial support. But RPS6 is on the minus strand. The plus-strand junctions might come from a paralog (processed pseudogene?) of RPS6 elsewhere on chr9, or from a segmental duplication... Or wait — hmm.\n\nNo, hold on. Let me reconsider. Ribosomal protein genes have tons of processed pseudogenes, and RPS6 itself is... hmm. But both strands at the same coordinates — that's the signature of an inverted duplication I noted before. Hmm, but there's another possibility: RPS6's annotation — let me check RPS6's introns. If RPS6's Ensembl introns are, say, (19376388,19376492)? The minus-strand version of that junction was classified as NOVEL (not in the intron set). But the plus-strand version (133349043-133349550, +1) of 133349042-133349549 was classified as ANNOTATED (it's in the top-annotated list!). So for RPL7A (plus strand at 133.35 Mb), intron (133349043,133349550) is annotated. And the minus-strand version of the same coordinates (133349042,133349549) is novel.\n\nSo the pattern is: at 133.35 Mb there's RPL7A (plus strand, ribosomal protein L7a). The minus-strand junctions are novel — they might be from RPL7A's antisense... or from a duplicated copy on the minus strand!\n\nHmm, wait, actually, hmm: ribosomal protein genes: RPL7A is 133.35 Mb. And there's a known RPL7A pseudogene... actually, let me check: is there a segmental duplication around 133.35 Mb? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about Region C: 111.66 Mb: GNG10/DNAJC25-GNG10 (strand +, 111661605-111670226, 3 exons). Junctions: (111661713,111664533): +16/-9; (111664590,111666814,+1,20) and (111664588,111666812,-1,5); (111666947,111669268): +20/-16. Depth: 111669000-111669499: 9308; 111669500-111669999: 24376(!); 111670000-111670499: 5478. Huge depth in the 111669-111670 kb region — GNG10's last exon? GNG10 encodes a G protein subunit gamma... hmm wait, depth 24376 in a 500bp bin = average depth ~49x. GNG10 is highly expressed.\n\nHmm interesting — but which junctions are annotated and which are novel? For GNG10: (111666947,111669268) appears in the annotated list (both +20 and -16) — so it's an annotated intron of GNG10. And (111661713,111664533): novel on both strands (+16, -9)... no wait, let me check: in probe15's annotated list: \"s=111666947 e=111669268 strand=+1 reads=20\" and \"-1 reads=16\" — annotated. In the novel list: (111664590,111666814,+1,20), (111661713,111664533,+1,16), (111661713,111664533,-1,9), (111664588,111666812,-1,5).\n\nHmm, GNG10 has 3 exons → 2 introns. If the annotated introns are (111666947,111669268) and another one... let me pull out GNG10's exon coordinates. Novel junctions (111661713,111664533) and (111664590,111666814): if GNG10's exons are [~111661605-111661712], [111664534-111664589], [111666815... wait, that doesn't match the annotated intron (111666947,111669268).\n\nLet me get GNG10's exact exon structure. If GNG10's exons are E1: 111661605-111661712, E2: 111664590-111666814, E3: 111669269-111670226, then introns are (111661713,111664589) and (111666815,111669268). But the novel junctions are (111661713,111664533) [ends 56 bp early] and (111664590,111666814) [hmm, that's exactly the E2 coordinates!]. And the annotated junction is (111666947,111669268). Confusing. Let me look at the actual annotation.\n\nActually, wait — I think this is the key: GNG10 is a gene with an alternative/cryptic 5' region... hmm, let me actually check the DNAJC25-GNG10 readthrough gene (strand +, 111631386-111670229, 3 exons).\n\nBefore speculating further, let me get the exact exon structures of the relevant genes: RPS6, RPL7A, GNG10, RPL35, HSPA5, SURF1/2, MED22.\n\nAnother interesting point: the depth bins for Region A (133.35 Mb, RPL7A): 133348500-133348999: 1047; 133349000-133349499: 451; 133349500-133349999: 3666; 133350000-133350499: 507; 133350500-133350999: 1498; 133351000-133351499: 856. Strong coverage across all of RPL7A.\n\nAnd Region B (RPS6): bins 19376500-19376999: 3918, 19378000-19378499: 3074, 19378500-19378999: 4095, plus 19379500-19379999: 736 (downstream of RPS6's end at 19380236? No, inside it), 19373500-19373999: 321 (inside DENND4C's last exon, probably).\n\nBoth RPS6 and RPL7A are ribosomal protein genes — classic highly expressed genes. Both regions show chains of novel junctions on both strands. Hmm.\n\nSo now, which junctions are the real \"two novel junctions supporting a cryptic exon\"?\n\nLet me get the exact exon structures. For RPS6 (minus strand), Ensembl exons (from gene_info). Then compute the annotated introns and compare with the observed junctions. The \"novel\" junctions that match the RPS6 coordinates exactly on the minus strand should be RPS6's annotated introns... but they were classified as novel — meaning they are not RPS6's introns in Ensembl 112. Unless the simulation used a different annotation for RPS6 (e.g., RPS6's RefSeq structure differs from Ensembl).\n\nHmm wait — actually, let me reconsider. Ensembl RPS6: I recall RPS6 has 5-6 exons... but here the annotation says RPS6 has 19 exons?? \"gene RPS6 ENSG00000137154 strand -1 span 19375715-19380236 exons 19\" — 19 exon rows = the union of exons across multiple transcripts (rows are transcript×exon pairs, and my gene_info collected them all, so duplicates across transcripts are counted multiple times).\n\nLet me dump the exact exon coordinates for RPS6's transcripts and RPL7A's transcripts, plus GNG10. And determine for each observed junction whether it matches any transcript's intron (with a ±2 bp tolerance for comparison).\n\nThen, the cryptic exon candidates: the interval between two novel junctions with high depth:\n- Region B (RPS6, minus strand): novel junctions on the minus strand: J(19376388,19376492,-1,9), J(19378514,19378706,-1,12), J(19378919,19379486,-1,14), J(19376649,19378364,-1,2). If RPS6's Ensembl structure differs from the simulated transcript... intervals between novel junctions: [19376493,19378513] (between J1 and J3, containing J2 inside) — hmm.\n- Depth bins: 19376500-19376999: 3918, 19378000-19378499: 3074, 19378500-19378999: 4095 — depth is high across 19376.5k-19379k, including the 19376649-19378364 interval (which is covered by bins 19376500-19376999 and 19378000-19378499).\n\nHmm, OK. What's needed here is: for each region, exact annotation exons/introns + exact depth profiles + junction strands. Then determine which intervals are: (a) between two novel junctions, (b) have high depth, (c) not annotated as exons in Ensembl 112.\n\nLet me write the next probe.\n"}}
