e with the GT donor is s=111661716. And importantly, this equals the annotated intron-1 donor (111661716).\n\nHmm wait — hmm, interesting: so J_left's donor = the annotated intron 1 donor (111661716). J_left's acceptor = 111664533 (AG at 111664532-111664533 ✓), which is novel (the cryptic exon's 5' end... hmm wait, on the plus strand, J_left = exon1→CE junction: donor = exon1's donor (annotated position), acceptor = the CE's 3' splice site (new position 111664533)).\n\nJ_right = CE→exon2 junction: donor 111664590 (GT ✓; CE end = 111664589), acceptor 111666814 (AG ✓ = annotated intron 1 acceptor, since annotated intron 1 = 111661716-111666814).\n\nSo cryptic exon = 111664534-111664589 (56 bp):\n- Left junction (exon1→CE): intron (111661716, 111664533) — novel ✓ (absent from annotation)\n- Right junction (CE→exon2): intron (111664590, 111666814) — novel ✓ (absent from annotation)\n\nBoth novel against Ensembl 112 ✓. CE interval per README: left_intron_end+1 = 111664534, right_intron_start-1 = 111664589 ✓ 56 bp.\n\n2. **Uniqueness**: the k-mers of GNG10's exon1, CE, and exon3 all have multiplicity 1 in the genome — GNG10 is single-copy! (Unlike RPL7A/RPS6 where the k-mers have >20 copies / multiple copies.) Therefore the GNG10 junction reads map uniquely — no ambiguity from duplications. The minus-strand junction \"copies\" in this region are probably misalignments... hmm wait, but if GNG10 is single-copy, why did 9 reads align on the minus strand at (111661713,111664533)? For a minus-strand alignment to pass mm≤3, the RC of the read must match the genome at those coordinates on the minus strand... the single-copy check says the exon sequence appears only once on the forward strand; its RC should also appear only once (as the RC of that locus, at the same location). For a minus-strand read alignment at the same coordinates: the read sequence = RC(spliced product) = an RC junction-spanning sequence. Could a forward-strand junction read's RC also align on the minus strand at shifted coordinates with mm≤3? Only if the spliced product is approximately palindromic — unlikely by chance...\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider: maybe those 9 \"minus-strand\" reads are actually forward-strand reads where my pipeline tried the RC first? No — my pipeline tries forward first, and only if forward fails does it try RC. Hmm, unless the forward placement was ambiguous/discarded... no, in probe14 the logic was: try forward; if unresolved, try RC. Actually looking at probe14: `rows=[(1,rseq[idx])]; if s2[idx]>=2: rows.append((-1,rc_row(rseq[idx])))`, then collect allp from both strands, take the best mm across both strands, and cluster. So if a forward read has a forward placement mm=1 and an RC placement mm=0 (by chance), it would pick RC! That's a flaw: the RC placement of a real forward read could occasionally be slightly better in mm. Hmm, for 107bp that would require an unlikely near-palindrome, but... with mm≤3 tolerance and microhomology shifts, maybe possible?\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. The CE sequence: 'AAGTTGCTGGGTAAACATGGGCTCAAACCAAGGAAAGTCAGGTAACCTGGGTGGAG' — and its flanks. Hmm, let me check whether the minus-strand junction reads have sequences that look like RC of the plus-strand junction sequences. If the minus-strand reads' sequences ≈ RC(plus-strand reads), then they're the same fragments and my strand assignment was just ambiguous. Let me pull out the read indices for both clusters and compare sequences.\n\nActually, hmm, wait: another possibility for minus-strand placements: the RC of the spliced product G[111661605..111661715]+CE+G[111666815..] could align to the genome on the minus strand if the genome contains the RC of that sequence — i.e., an inverted copy. But the k-mer multiplicity check says GNG10's exon k-mers are single-copy... on the forward strand. An inverted copy elsewhere would contain the RC of the exon sequence — the k-mer lookup for a forward exon k-mer wouldn't hit it (different strand). My pos_dict inde