by the 49-read row ✓. And the minus-strand clusters (111661713,111664533,-1,9) and (111664588,111666812,-1,5) exist as rows.\n\nIs this confusing? Somewhat — the raw microhomology-shifted cluster rows are redundant with the refined junctions. Better approach: mark rows superseded by refinement. Options: (a) remove the superseded raw cluster rows entirely; (b) add a note \"superseded by refined junction (microhomology)\". I'll go with (b)-style: add a note to the raw cluster row corresponding to the refined junction saying it's a microhomology-shifted alignment variant of the refined cryptic junction. Which raw clusters correspond? The candidate's raw clusters: cand.left[:2]=(111661713,111664533) strand +, cand.right[:2]=(111664590,111666814) +. The minus-strand ones (111661713,111664533,-) and (111664588,111666812,-) are RC-strand alignments of reads from the same junction. Mark both:\n- (111661713,111664533,+): \"microhomology-shifted variant of cryptic_exon_left; superseded by (111661716,111664536)\"\n- (111661713,111664533,-): \"antisense-cDNA reads of the cryptic_exon_left junction (microhomology-shifted coordinates)\"\n- (111664588,111666812,-): same for right.\nSimplest generic implementation: in write_outputs, any raw jrow whose coordinates are within ±4 of either refined junction (either strand) gets note = \"microhomology variant of <role>; superseded\" and gene=GNG10. Let me implement proximity-based marking.\n\n4. Also fix: the chrom column for every row.\n\n5. The \"0 reads span constitutive intron 1, 111661716-111666814\" in expression_evidence — good.\n\nLet me patch write_outputs: add chrom to rows + supersede marking. Then rerun just the output stage… rerunning the whole pipeline takes 3.5 minutes; acceptable. Actually — I can make reruns faster by having the script cache intermediate results, but… let me keep it simple: just rerun the whole thing (204 seconds).\n\nWait — one more thing, also double-check the report.md that was written in the previous run (before this fix) — it'll be regenerated on the rerun. And confirm that the left junction's gene row is labeled as GNG10 ✓.\n\nPatch details inside write_outputs:\n- For all row tuples: add r[\"chrom\"]/chrom as the first element.\n- Before the jrows loop, build a supersede map: for each raw jrow, if |start - li[0]|<=4 and |end - li[1]|<=4 → note about left; elif |start - ri[0]|<=4 and |end - ri[1]|<=4 → right. Apply to both strands. Set gene=gene_name, reads unchanged, role=\"microhomology_variant\".\n- Also make sure the refined row that exists in jrows (the right junction's raw cluster) gets the refined row (already handled by the refined_juncs check).\n\nLet me write the patch.\n"}}
